{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>And Elohim met Balaam.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): the language is that of occurrence (מקרה), for in honor of Israel the divine speech came to him by way of an occurrent event, and therefore Scripture says of it ויבא אלהים אל בלעם — 'and Elohim came to Balaam' — for we do not find this expression regarding any prophet except one who had not attained the rank of prophet, such as Avimelekh and Lavan. Furthermore, the expression ויקר — 'and he met' — includes the idea that there befell Balaam an occurrence like those occurrences that happen to those who possess the holy spirit when they seclude themselves and clothe themselves in unification and stand trembling. And afterward Scripture says ויקר ה' אל בלעם — 'and YHWH met Balaam' — because his apprehension grew great and his matter rose to prophesy in the unique Name, like the prophecy of Moses, and by this he recognized that there was no possibility of cursing them at all, for the attribute of mercy was with them to deliver them. At first, when he heard from the attribute of judgment, he hoped to find an opening for his curse, that the attribute of judgment might be stretched out against them; but once he heard from the attribute of mercy, his hope was dashed. And this great ascent that he reached was only temporary, in honor of Israel, so that the nations would not have an opening to say on the day of judgment: had we had prophets like Israel, we would have returned to the better path; and so that Israel would have an opening against the nations of the world, and the nations would hear the wondrous promises concerning Israel from the mouth of their own prophet, who is the adversary of Israel. And behold, once he completed his prophecy with those promises, prophecy departed from him and he returned to his divinations, as it says (Joshua 13:22): ״ואת בלעם בן בעור הקוסם הרגו בחרב״ — 'and Balaam son of Beor the diviner they slew with the sword' — and this indicates that he died a diviner, and prophecy did not continue for him thereafter." "<b>And by way of the Midrash:</b> ויקר אלהים אל בלעם — 'And Elohim met Balaam.' What is the difference between the prophets of Israel and the prophets of the nations of the world? The prophets of Israel — revelation came to them by day, as it says (Exodus 6:28): ״ביום דבר ה' אל משה״ — 'on the day HaShem spoke to Moses.' The prophets of the nations of the world — by night, as it says (Genesis 31:24): ״ויבא אלהים אל לבן הארמי בחלום הלילה״ — 'and Elohim came to Lavan the Aramean in a dream of the night'; (Genesis 20:3): ״ויבא אלהים אל אבימלך בחלום הלילה״ — 'and Elohim came to Avimelekh in a dream of the night'; and 'and Elohim came to Balaam — at night.' A parable: to a king who had a wife and a concubine. When he came to the wife, he was not ashamed to come to her by day before everyone; but when he came to the concubine, he would come stealthily by night. The prophets of Israel — revelation comes to them in complete speech, as it says (Leviticus 1:1): ״ויקרא אל משה״ — 'and He called to Moses.' The prophets of the nations of the world — in half speech, as it says: ״ויקר אלהים אל בלעם״ — 'and Elohim met Balaam.' The prophets of Israel — He speaks with them in language of purity and holiness, as it says (Exodus 19:3): ״ויקרא ה' למשה״ — 'and YHWH called to Moses'; as you say (Isaiah 6:3): ״וקרא זה אל זה ואמר קדוש״ — 'and one called to the other and said, Holy.' The prophets of the nations of the world — in language of impurity and emission, as it says: ״ויקר אלהים אל בלעם״ — 'and Elohim met Balaam.' A parable — to what may the matter be compared? To two men seeking an audience at the king's gate, one the king's beloved and one a leper. Said the king: 'My beloved may enter; the leper may not enter, lest he defile my palace — rather, I shall go out to meet him,' as it says: ״ויקר אלהים אל בלעם״ — He went out to meet him." "<b>The seven altars have I arranged.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): the seven altars that Balak built — I arranged offerings upon them, so that they might find favor before You. And therefore he mentioned them with the definite article (הידיעה)." "<b>And by way of the Midrash:</b> Therefore he mentioned them with the definite article (הידיעה), because we find righteous men who built altars from the day the world was created until Balaam, and these are they: Adam, Cain, Abel, Noah, Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob — each one built his own, and Balaam built corresponding to all of them. Therefore he mentioned them with the definite article (ה״א הידיעה)." "<b>And by way of reason (sekhel):</b> The seven altars have I arranged — Scripture seized upon the reckoning of seven because it is the intermediate cycle. For it is known that there are four cycles: the cycle of the day, the week, the month, and the year. The cycle of the day is that the sun completes its circuit in its sphere in one day from east to west, and this is the first cycle, which is the daily motion; and when the day is completed it begins again, and so for every single day. And there is another cycle, the cycle of the week: for when the week is completed they begin again and count another week. And there is yet another cycle, the cycle of the moon in each and every month; and the intermediate cycle that lies between the day and the month is the cycle of the seven days of the week. Therefore the cycle of seven is intermediate between the solar day and the lunar month. And since all the affairs of nature follow after this intermediate cycle, and the affairs of the Torah always draw near to the affairs of nature, therefore many commandments in the Torah come in the reckoning of seven, such as the days of Passover and the like, because it is the reckoning of the intermediate cycle, corresponding to the seven wandering stars upon which all the pillars of nature rest. For one cannot say that the days of Passover should be a full year, which is the fourth cycle — when the year is completed they begin again and count another year — for the matter would not be discernible; and if it were but a single day, the time would be too brief; and if it were a single month, it would be too much. Therefore the Torah desired to draw near to nature and make the time of Passover in the reckoning of the intermediate and mean cycle. And according to this reasoning, Balaam made the altars seven, to allude to this intermediate cycle, for he was entirely a man of nature and wise in the wisdom of the constellations. This is the opinion of the Rambam, of blessed memory." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: 'the seven altars have I arranged' — that he made seven of them and that he mentioned them with the definite article ה — all was under complete providence and a known intention. For Balaam, in his great desire to curse, wished to draw down will from the pure Thought to all the seven and to infuse power in them, and it did not suffice him to make one altar, so he made seven altars, in order that all the attributes might effect his will and that his thought might suffice for the drawing down of the will. And his offerings rose in the three places, as explained above (42)." "<b>And behold,</b> these seven altars are the seven days of Creation and the seven lamps of the Menorah, and they are called צרור החיים, for they are a distinct matter above that branches out below, according to the recipients. And this is the meaning of the verse: 'the seven altars of above have I arranged below.'" "<b>And the sage</b> Rabbi Abraham, of blessed memory, in his pure rhetoric alluded to this — if you attend to it — namely what he said: ובתת שלם לשלם אז תתחדש רוח בינה."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {7 "The phrase ובתת שלם לשלם אז תתחדש רוח בינה appears to be a quotation from R. Abraham ibn Ezra (possibly in poetic or rhetorical form); left untranslated in Hebrew as it stands as a cited dictum whose exact source is not identified."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:35:43.172378229Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}